# surfd admin panel -- the systemctl grant the panel needs.
#
# THIS FILE IS A RECORD OF THE GRANT ON THE PI, NOT AN INSTALLER.
#
# /etc/sudoers.d/ftesurf (0440 root:root, dated Sep 8 17:09) grants the proto
# user these commands NOPASSWD. `sudo -n -l` as proto shows what is really
# there. Do NOT install this file as a second grant -- two sudoers files granting
# overlapping commands is how a later edit removes one and leaves the other,
# and the panel keeps working for reasons nobody can find.
#
# FIFTEEN COMMANDS HERE; THE PI HELD NINE UNTIL QC BUILD 57. The six lines for
# ftesurf@4 and ftesurf@5, the two bhop lobbies, are APPENDED to the existing
# file by a human with root -- see "ADDING A LOBBY" at the bottom. Until they
# are, the panel and `build.ps1 -Pi` get "a password is required" for lobbies
# 4 and 5 only.
#
# It is kept in the repo because the grant itself is not in version control
# anywhere, and "what privilege does the web panel hold" is exactly the thing
# that should not live only in a root-owned file on one SBC.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SPELLING IS LOAD-BEARING: ftesurf@1, NOT ftesurf@1.service
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# sudo matches a granted command against the argument vector LITERALLY. systemd
# treats `ftesurf@1` and `ftesurf@1.service` as the same unit, so the two look
# interchangeable -- and they are, to systemctl. They are not to sudo.
#
#     sudo -n systemctl restart ftesurf@1           -> allowed (matches)
#     sudo -n systemctl restart ftesurf@1.service   -> "a password is required"
#
# The second failure is indistinguishable from "the sudoers line was never
# installed", which is the wrong conclusion and sends you to a human with root
# for a file that is already there. admin.py's systemctl() and build.ps1 -Pi
# both build the suffix-less form deliberately and say so where they do it.
#
# If this grant is ever rewritten WITH the suffix, change admin.py and
# build.ps1 to match. Do not add a second line covering the other spelling.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IT GRANTS, and why each restriction is worth keeping
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   * Fifteen exact commands. No wildcards anywhere, so sudo cannot be talked
#     into restarting nginx, ssh or anything else on the box.
#   * ftesurf@1..5 only. surfd.service is NOT in the list: a panel that can
#     restart the process it runs inside can hang itself up mid-request with no
#     way back.
#   * NOPASSWD, because there is no terminal to type a password at. admin.py
#     calls `sudo -n` and reports the refusal plainly rather than hanging.
#   * No `systemctl is-active`. admin.py reads unit state WITHOUT sudo, because
#     querying needs no privilege. Only the three state-changing verbs are
#     granted. If unit state ever shows blank in the panel, that is a systemd
#     question, not a reason to widen this file.
#
# THE HONEST RISK. Anyone who takes a session on the public panel can stop the
# game servers. That is the intended feature -- an admin panel that cannot stop
# a server is not an admin panel -- and it is bounded: five units, no shell,
# no other service, no package manager, no file writes. The blast radius is
# "the lobbies go down", recoverable from any ssh session in seconds.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADDING A LOBBY -- append to the existing file, never copy over it
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#     sudo visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/ftesurf   # append the unit's three lines
#     sudo visudo -c                          # MUST print "parsed OK"
#     sudo -n -l                              # as proto: the new lines listed
#     sudo systemctl enable --now ftesurf@4 ftesurf@5
#
# visudo -f checks the syntax before it saves, which an ordinary editor does
# not. Enable the unit only once its rotation maps are on the Pi: a lobby
# started before its default map is there has no map to load. `enable` is not
# in this grant and needs the password -- a one-off per new lobby, not
# something the panel ever does.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ONLY IF THE EXISTING GRANT IS EVER LOST
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#     sudo cp /srv/nvme/surfd/surfd-admin.sudoers /etc/sudoers.d/ftesurf
#     sudo chmod 0440 /etc/sudoers.d/ftesurf
#     sudo visudo -c                      # MUST print "parsed OK"
#
# RUN visudo -c. A syntax error in a sudoers file can lock every user out of
# sudo on the machine. `visudo -c` parses every file in /etc/sudoers.d and is
# the whole safety net.

proto ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl start ftesurf@1
proto ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl start ftesurf@2
proto ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl start ftesurf@3
proto ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl start ftesurf@4
proto ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl start ftesurf@5

proto ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl stop ftesurf@1
proto ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl stop ftesurf@2
proto ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl stop ftesurf@3
proto ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl stop ftesurf@4
proto ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl stop ftesurf@5

proto ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart ftesurf@1
proto ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart ftesurf@2
proto ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart ftesurf@3
proto ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart ftesurf@4
proto ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart ftesurf@5
